{{also|Brit|Brit.|Brit-|břit}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/bɹɪt/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪt|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|brytten}}, {{m|enm|brutten}}, from {{inh|en|ang|brittian}}, {{m|ang|bryttian|t=to divide, dispense, distribute, rule over, possess, enjoy the use of}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*brutjaną|t=to break, divide}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰrewd-|t=to break}}. Cognate with {{cog|is|brytja|t=to chop up, break in pieces, slaughter}}, {{cog|sv|bryta|t=to break, fracture, cut off}}, {{cog|da|bryde|t=to break}}, and outside the Germanic family with {{cog|sq|brydh|t=I make crumbly, friable, soft}}. Related to {{m+|ang|brytta|t=dispenser, giver, author, governor, prince}}, {{m+|ang|brēotan|t=to break in pieces, hew down, demolish, destroy, kill}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|britt}}
* {{l|en|brite}} {{q|dialectal}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[break]] in pieces; [[divide]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[bruise]]; [[indent]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[fall]] out or [[shatter]] (as overripe hops or grain).
# {{lb|en|intransitive|dialectal}} To [[fade]] away; [[alter]].

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|britten}}
* {{l|en|brittle}}

===Etymology 2===
Probably from {{inh|en|enm|bret}} or {{m|en|birt}}, applied to a different kind of fish. See {{m|en|bret}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|britt}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|brit}}

# One of the young of [[herring]]s, [[sprat]]s, etc.
# One of the tiny [[crustacean]]s, of the [[genus]] ''[[Calanus]]'', that are part of the [[diet]] of [[right whale]]s.
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|text=The edges of these bones are fringed with hairy fibres, through which the Right Whale strains the water, and in whose intricacies he retains the small fish, when openmouthed he goes through the seas of '''brit''' in feeding time.}}

===Etymology 3===
Short for {{m|en|brit milah}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|bris}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# [[brit milah]]

